For action values, the off-policy definition of the _n_-step return is a little different because the first action does not play a role in the importance sampling. That first action is the one being learned; it does not matter if it was unlikely or even impossible under the target policy--it has been taken and now full unit weight must be given to the reward and state that follows it. Importance sampling will apply only to the actions that follow it.

First note that for action values the _n_-step _on-policy_ return ending at horizon \(h\), expectation form (7.7), can be written recursively just as in (7.12), except that for action values the recursion ends with \(G_{h:h}\doteq\bar{V}_{h-1}(S_{h})\) as in (7.8). An off-policy form with control variates is

\[G_{t:h} \doteq R_{t+1}+\gamma\Big{(}\rho_{t+1}G_{t+1:h}+\bar{V}_{h-1}(S_{t+1})- \rho_{t+1}Q_{h-1}(S_{t+1},A_{t+1})\Big{)},\] \[=R_{t+1}+\gamma\rho_{t+1}\Big{(}G_{t+1:h}-Q_{h-1}(S_{t+1},A_{t+1} )\Big{)}+\gamma\bar{V}_{h-1}(S_{t+1}),\ \ t<h\leq T. \tag{7.14}\]

If \(h<T\), then the recursion ends with \(G_{h:h}\doteq Q_{h-1}(S_{h},A_{h})\), whereas, if \(h\geq T\), the recursion ends with and \(G_{T-1:h}\doteq R_{T}\). The resultant prediction algorithm (after combining with (7.5)) is analogous to Expected Sarsa.

